{"surah": 65, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلنَّبِىُّ إِذَا طَلَّقْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَطَلِّقُوهُنَّ لِعِدَّتِهِنَّ وَأَحْصُوا۟ ٱلْعِدَّةَۖ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ رَبَّكُمْۖ لَا تُخْرِجُوهُنَّ مِنۢ بُيُوتِهِنَّ وَلَا يَخْرُجْنَ إِلَّآ أَن يَأْتِينَ بِفَٰحِشَةٍ مُّبَيِّنَةٍۚ وَتِلْكَ حُدُودُ ٱللَّهِۚ وَمَن يَتَعَدَّ حُدُودَ ٱللَّهِ فَقَدْ ظَلَمَ نَفْسَهُۥۚ لَا تَدْرِى لَعَلَّ ٱللَّهَ يُحْدِثُ بَعْدَ ذَٰلِكَ أَمْرًا ", "translation": "O Prophet, when you [Muslims] divorce women, divorce them for [the commencement of] their waiting period and keep count of the waiting period,[1] and fear Allāh, your Lord. Do not turn them out of their [husbands'] houses, nor should they [themselves] leave [during that period] unless they are committing a clear immorality. And those are the limits [set by] Allāh. And whoever transgresses the limits of Allāh has certainly wronged himself. You know not; perhaps Allāh will bring about after that a [different] matter.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلنَّبِىُّ", "translit": "ُّàِbَّnlٱ", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "طَلَّقْتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtْqَّlَṭ", "root": "طلق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلنِّسَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَsِّnlٱ", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَطَلِّقُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُqِّlَṭَf", "root": "طلق"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِعِدَّتِهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhِtَّdِʿعِl", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَأَحْصُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُṣْḥَaʾَw", "root": "حصي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْعِدَّةَ", "translit": "َẗَّdِʿعْlٱ", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "رَبَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "تُخْرِجُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُjgِrْhkُt", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بُيُوتِهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhِtwُyُb", "root": "بيت"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَخْرُجْنَ", "translit": "َnْjgُrْhkَy", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَأْتِينَ", "translit": "َnyِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِفَٰحِشَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَhsِḥَٰfِb", "root": "فحش"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مُّبَيِّنَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَnِّyَbُّm", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "وَتِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِtَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "حُدُودُ", "translit": "ُdwُdُḥ", "root": "حدد"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "يَتَعَدَّ", "translit": "َّdَʿعَtَy", "root": "عدو"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "حُدُودَ", "translit": "َdwُdُḥ", "root": "حدد"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "فَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ظَلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlَẓ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "نَفْسَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "تَدْرِى", "translit": "àِrْdَt", "root": "دري"}, {"id": 36, "arabic": "لَعَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَʿعَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 37, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 38, "arabic": "يُحْدِثُ", "translit": "ُhtِdْḥُy", "root": "حدث"}, {"id": 39, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 40, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 41, "arabic": "أَمْرًا", "translit": "āًrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "فَإِذَا بَلَغْنَ أَجَلَهُنَّ فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ بِمَعْرُوفٍ أَوْ فَارِقُوهُنَّ بِمَعْرُوفٍ وَأَشْهِدُوا۟ ذَوَىْ عَدْلٍ مِّنكُمْ وَأَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلشَّهَٰدَةَ لِلَّهِۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ يُوعَظُ بِهِۦ مَن كَانَ يُؤْمِنُ بِٱللَّهِ وَٱلْيَوْمِ ٱلْءَاخِرِۚ وَمَن يَتَّقِ ٱللَّهَ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ مَخْرَجًا ", "translation": "And when they have [nearly] fulfilled their term, either retain them according to acceptable terms or part with them according to acceptable terms. And bring to witness two just men from among you and establish the testimony for [the acceptance of] Allāh. That is instructed to whoever should believe in Allāh and the Last Day. And whoever fears Allāh - He will make for him a way out[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَلَغْنَ", "translit": "َnْغَlَb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَجَلَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَمْسِكُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُkِsْmَaʾَf", "root": "مسك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِمَعْرُوفٍ", "translit": "ٍfwُrْʿعَmِb", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَارِقُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُqِrāَf", "root": "فرق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِمَعْرُوفٍ", "translit": "ٍfwُrْʿعَmِb", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَشْهِدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdِhْhsَaʾَw", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذَوَىْ", "translit": "ْàَwَhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَدْلٍ", "translit": "ٍlْdَʿع", "root": "عدل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِّنكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُknِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَأَقِيمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmyِqَaʾَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلشَّهَٰدَةَ", "translit": "َẗَdَٰhَّhslٱ", "root": "شهد"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُوعَظُ", "translit": "ُẓَʿعwُy", "root": "وعظ"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُ", "translit": "ُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "وَٱلْيَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَyْlٱَw", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرِ", "translit": "ِrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "يَتَّقِ", "translit": "ِqَّtَy", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "يَجْعَل", "translit": "lَʿعْjgَy", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "مَخْرَجًا", "translit": "āًjgَrْhkَm", "root": "خرج"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَيَرْزُقْهُ مِنْ حَيْثُ لَا يَحْتَسِبُۚ وَمَن يَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ فَهُوَ حَسْبُهُۥٓۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ بَٰلِغُ أَمْرِهِۦۚ قَدْ جَعَلَ ٱللَّهُ لِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدْرًا ", "translation": "And will provide for him from where he does not expect. And whoever relies upon Allāh - then He is sufficient for him. Indeed, Allāh will accomplish His purpose. Allāh has already set for everything a [decreed] extent.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَيَرْزُقْهُ", "translit": "ُhْqُzْrَyَw", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَيْثُ", "translit": "ُhtْyَḥ", "root": "حيث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَحْتَسِبُ", "translit": "ُbِsَtْḥَy", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَتَوَكَّلْ", "translit": "ْlَّkَwَtَy", "root": "وكل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "حَسْبُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُbْsَḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَٰلِغُ", "translit": "ُغِlَٰb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَمْرِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "لِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "قَدْرًا", "translit": "āًrْdَq", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "وَٱلَّٰٓـِٔى يَئِسْنَ مِنَ ٱلْمَحِيضِ مِن نِّسَآئِكُمْ إِنِ ٱرْتَبْتُمْ فَعِدَّتُهُنَّ ثَلَٰثَةُ أَشْهُرٍ وَٱلَّٰٓـِٔى لَمْ يَحِضْنَۚ وَأُو۟لَٰتُ ٱلْأَحْمَالِ أَجَلُهُنَّ أَن يَضَعْنَ حَمْلَهُنَّۚ وَمَن يَتَّقِ ٱللَّهَ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ مِنْ أَمْرِهِۦ يُسْرًا ", "translation": "And those who no longer expect menstruation among your women - if you doubt, then their period is three months, and [also for] those who have not menstruated. And for those who are pregnant, their term is until they give birth.[1] And whoever fears Allāh - He will make for him of his matter ease.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّٰٓـِٔى", "translit": "àِٔـَّٰٓlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَئِسْنَ", "translit": "َnْsِyʾَy", "root": "ياس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَحِيضِ", "translit": "ِḍyِḥَmْlٱ", "root": "حيض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "نِّسَآئِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِyʾٓāَsِّn", "root": "نسو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِنِ", "translit": "ِnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱرْتَبْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْbَtْrٱ", "root": "ريب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَعِدَّتُهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhُtَّdِʿعَf", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ثَلَٰثَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَhtَٰlَht", "root": "ثلث"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَشْهُرٍ", "translit": "ٍrُhْhsَaʾ", "root": "شهر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَٱلَّٰٓـِٔى", "translit": "àِٔـَّٰٓlٱَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَحِضْنَ", "translit": "َnْḍِḥَy", "root": "حيض"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "وَأُو۟لَٰتُ", "translit": "ُtَٰl۟wُaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْأَحْمَالِ", "translit": "ِlāَmْḥَaʾْlٱ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "أَجَلُهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhُlَjgَaʾ", "root": "اجل"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "يَضَعْنَ", "translit": "َnْʿعَḍَy", "root": "وضع"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "حَمْلَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَlْmَḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "يَتَّقِ", "translit": "ِqَّtَy", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "يَجْعَل", "translit": "lَʿعْjgَy", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "لَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "أَمْرِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "يُسْرًا", "translit": "āًrْsُy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ أَمْرُ ٱللَّهِ أَنزَلَهُۥٓ إِلَيْكُمْۚ وَمَن يَتَّقِ ٱللَّهَ يُكَفِّرْ عَنْهُ سَيِّـَٔاتِهِۦ وَيُعْظِمْ لَهُۥٓ أَجْرًا ", "translation": "That is the command of Allāh, which He has sent down to you; and whoever fears Allāh - He will remove for him his misdeeds and make great for him his reward.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَنزَلَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَتَّقِ", "translit": "ِqَّtَy", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُكَفِّرْ", "translit": "ْrِّfَkُy", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "سَيِّـَٔاتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtāَٔـِّyَs", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَيُعْظِمْ", "translit": "ْmِẓْʿعُyَw", "root": "عظم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَجْرًا", "translit": "āًrْjgَaʾ", "root": "اجر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "أَسْكِنُوهُنَّ مِنْ حَيْثُ سَكَنتُم مِّن وُجْدِكُمْ وَلَا تُضَآرُّوهُنَّ لِتُضَيِّقُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّۚ وَإِن كُنَّ أُو۟لَٰتِ حَمْلٍ فَأَنفِقُوا۟ عَلَيْهِنَّ حَتَّىٰ يَضَعْنَ حَمْلَهُنَّۚ فَإِنْ أَرْضَعْنَ لَكُمْ فَـَٔاتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّۖ وَأْتَمِرُوا۟ بَيْنَكُم بِمَعْرُوفٍۖ وَإِن تَعَاسَرْتُمْ فَسَتُرْضِعُ لَهُۥٓ أُخْرَىٰ ", "translation": "Lodge them[1] [in a section] of where you dwell out of your means and do not harm them in order to oppress them.[2] And if they should be pregnant, then spend on them until they give birth. And if they breastfeed for you, then give them their payment and confer among yourselves in the acceptable way; but if you are in discord, then there may breastfeed for him [i.e., the father] another woman.[3]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَسْكِنُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُnِkْsَaʾ", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "حَيْثُ", "translit": "ُhtْyَḥ", "root": "حيث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَكَنتُم", "translit": "mُtnَkَs", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وُجْدِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِdْjgُw", "root": "وجد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُضَآرُّوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُّrٓāَḍُt", "root": "ضرر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "لِتُضَيِّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِّyَḍُtِl", "root": "ضيق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "كُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "حَمْلٍ", "translit": "ٍlْmَḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "فَأَنفِقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqِfnَaʾَf", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "حَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَḥ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "يَضَعْنَ", "translit": "َnْʿعَḍَy", "root": "وضع"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "حَمْلَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَlْmَḥ", "root": "حمل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "فَإِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَرْضَعْنَ", "translit": "َnْʿعَḍْrَaʾ", "root": "رضع"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "فَـَٔاتُوهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhwُtāَٔـَf", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أُجُورَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَrwُjgُaʾ", "root": "اجر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "وَأْتَمِرُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُrِmَtْaʾَw", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "بَيْنَكُم", "translit": "mُkَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "بِمَعْرُوفٍ", "translit": "ٍfwُrْʿعَmِb", "root": "عرف"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "وَإِن", "translit": "nِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "تَعَاسَرْتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtْrَsāَʿعَt", "root": "عسر"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "فَسَتُرْضِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِḍْrُtَsَf", "root": "رضع"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "لَهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "أُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "لِيُنفِقْ ذُو سَعَةٍ مِّن سَعَتِهِۦۖ وَمَن قُدِرَ عَلَيْهِ رِزْقُهُۥ فَلْيُنفِقْ مِمَّآ ءَاتَىٰهُ ٱللَّهُۚ لَا يُكَلِّفُ ٱللَّهُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا مَآ ءَاتَىٰهَاۚ سَيَجْعَلُ ٱللَّهُ بَعْدَ عُسْرٍ يُسْرًا ", "translation": "Let a man of wealth spend from his wealth, and he whose provision is restricted - let him spend from what Allāh has given him. Allāh does not charge a soul except [according to] what He has given it. Allāh will bring about, after hardship, ease [i.e., relief].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لِيُنفِقْ", "translit": "ْqِfnُyِl", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذُو", "translit": "wُhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "سَعَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَʿعَs", "root": "وسع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَعَتِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِtَʿعَs", "root": "وسع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قُدِرَ", "translit": "َrِdُq", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رِزْقُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَلْيُنفِقْ", "translit": "ْqِfnُyْlَf", "root": "نفق"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِمَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ءَاتَىٰهُ", "translit": "ُhٰàَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يُكَلِّفُ", "translit": "ُfِّlَkُy", "root": "كلف"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "نَفْسًا", "translit": "āًsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ءَاتَىٰهَا", "translit": "āَhٰàَtāَء", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "سَيَجْعَلُ", "translit": "ُlَʿعْjgَyَs", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "بَعْدَ", "translit": "َdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "عُسْرٍ", "translit": "ٍrْsُʿع", "root": "عسر"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "يُسْرًا", "translit": "āًrْsُy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَكَأَيِّن مِّن قَرْيَةٍ عَتَتْ عَنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّهَا وَرُسُلِهِۦ فَحَاسَبْنَٰهَا حِسَابًا شَدِيدًا وَعَذَّبْنَٰهَا عَذَابًا نُّكْرًا ", "translation": "And how many a city was insolent toward the command of its Lord and His messengers, so We took it to severe account and punished it with a terrible punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَأَيِّن", "translit": "nِّyَaʾَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَرْيَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَyْrَq", "root": "قري"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَتَتْ", "translit": "ْtَtَʿع", "root": "عتو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَنْ", "translit": "ْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَمْرِ", "translit": "ِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّهَا", "translit": "āَhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَرُسُلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlُsُrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَحَاسَبْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْbَsāَḥَf", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "حِسَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَsِḥ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "شَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَعَذَّبْنَٰهَا", "translit": "āَhَٰnْbَّhdَʿعَw", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "نُّكْرًا", "translit": "āًrْkُّn", "root": "نكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "فَذَاقَتْ وَبَالَ أَمْرِهَا وَكَانَ عَٰقِبَةُ أَمْرِهَا خُسْرًا ", "translation": "And it tasted the bad consequence of its affair [i.e., rebellion], and the outcome of its affair was loss.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَذَاقَتْ", "translit": "ْtَqāَhdَf", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَبَالَ", "translit": "َlāَbَw", "root": "وبل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَمْرِهَا", "translit": "āَhِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَw", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَٰقِبَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَbِqَٰʿع", "root": "عقب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَمْرِهَا", "translit": "āَhِrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "خُسْرًا", "translit": "āًrْsُhk", "root": "خسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "أَعَدَّ ٱللَّهُ لَهُمْ عَذَابًا شَدِيدًاۖ فَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ يَٰٓأُو۟لِى ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ۚ قَدْ أَنزَلَ ٱللَّهُ إِلَيْكُمْ ذِكْرًا ", "translation": "Allāh has prepared for them a severe punishment; so fear Allāh, O you of understanding who have believed. Allāh has sent down to you a message [i.e., the Qur’ān].[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَعَدَّ", "translit": "َّdَʿعَaʾ", "root": "عدد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَذَابًا", "translit": "āًbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَدِيدًا", "translit": "āًdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱتَّقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqَّtٱَf", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يَٰٓأُو۟لِى", "translit": "àِl۟wُaʾَٰٓy", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَلْبَٰبِ", "translit": "ِbَٰbْlَaʾْlٱ", "root": "لبب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ذِكْرًا", "translit": "āًrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "رَّسُولًا يَتْلُوا۟ عَلَيْكُمْ ءَايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ مُبَيِّنَٰتٍ لِّيُخْرِجَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِۚ وَمَن يُؤْمِنۢ بِٱللَّهِ وَيَعْمَلْ صَٰلِحًا يُدْخِلْهُ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ خَٰلِدِينَ فِيهَآ أَبَدًاۖ قَدْ أَحْسَنَ ٱللَّهُ لَهُۥ رِزْقًا ", "translation": "[He sent] a Messenger [i.e., Muḥammad (ﷺ)] reciting to you the distinct verses of Allāh that He may bring out those who believe and do righteous deeds from darknesses into the light. And whoever believes in Allāh and does righteousness - He will admit him into gardens beneath which rivers flow to abide therein forever. Allāh will have perfected for him a provision.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "رَّسُولًا", "translit": "āًlwُsَّr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَتْلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlْtَy", "root": "تلو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُبَيِّنَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰnِّyَbُm", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لِّيُخْرِجَ", "translit": "َjgِrْhkُyِّl", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَعَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿعَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰḥِlَّٰṣlٱ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰmُlُّẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلنُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَيَعْمَلْ", "translit": "ْlَmْʿعَyَw", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "صَٰلِحًا", "translit": "āًḥِlَٰṣ", "root": "صلح"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يُدْخِلْهُ", "translit": "ُhْlِhkْdُy", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "خَٰلِدِينَ", "translit": "َnyِdِlَٰhk", "root": "خلد"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "فِيهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَبَدًا", "translit": "āًdَbَaʾ", "root": "ابد"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "أَحْسَنَ", "translit": "َnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "رِزْقًا", "translit": "āًqْzِr", "root": "رزق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ سَبْعَ سَمَٰوَٰتٍ وَمِنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ مِثْلَهُنَّ يَتَنَزَّلُ ٱلْأَمْرُ بَيْنَهُنَّ لِتَعْلَمُوٓا۟ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ وَأَنَّ ٱللَّهَ قَدْ أَحَاطَ بِكُلِّ شَىْءٍ عِلْمًۢا ", "translation": "It is Allāh who has created seven heavens and of the earth, the like of them.[1] [His] command descends among them so you may know that Allāh is over all things competent and that Allāh has encompassed all things in knowledge.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "سَبْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْbَs", "root": "سبع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَمَٰوَٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَٰwَٰmَs", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِثْلَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَlْhtِm", "root": "مثل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَتَنَزَّلُ", "translit": "ُlَّzَnَtَy", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْأَمْرُ", "translit": "ُrْmَaʾْlٱ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُنَّ", "translit": "َّnُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِتَعْلَمُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُmَlْʿعَtِl", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "كُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قَدِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِdَq", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "قَدْ", "translit": "ْdَq", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "أَحَاطَ", "translit": "َṭāَḥَaʾ", "root": "حوط"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "بِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِb", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "عِلْمًۢا", "translit": "āًۢmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "باختلاف الأحوال والأشخاص فيكون الحد ضروريا فلذلك قال الله تعالى {ومن يتعد حدود الله فقد ظلم نفسه} ولأن أصول هذه المعاني لا تختلف فيها الشرائع وإنما تختلف في وجوه التحديد كما يحد شرع عيسى ابن مريم عليه السلام تحريم الخمر بالسكر وقد حده شرعنا بكونه من جنس المسكر لأن قليله يدعو إلى كثير والداخل في الحدود داخل في التحريم بحكم الجنس كما دخل أصل المعنى بالجملة الأصلية فهذا مثال واحد لحكمة خفية من حكم النقدين فينبغي أن يعتبر شكر النعمة وكفرانها بهذا المثال فكل ما خلق لحكمة فينبغي أن يصرف عنها ولا يعرف هذا إلا من قد عرف الحكمة {ومن يؤت الحكمة فقد أوتى", "english": "He does not eat it if he is in need, nor does he make it a commodity of trade. But if he makes it a commodity of trade, let him sell it to one who seeks it in exchange for something other than food, being in need of that other thing. As for one who seeks it in exchange for that very same food, he too is independent of it. For this reason, the curse upon the hoarder (muḥtakir) has come down in the Sacred Law, and therein are the severities we have mentioned in the Book of the Etiquettes of Earning. Yes, the seller of wheat for dates is excused, for neither one of them takes the place of the other in the intended purpose. But the seller of a ṣāʿ of wheat for a ṣāʿ of the same wheat is not excused; rather, he is a trifler, and thus needs no prohibition, for souls do not permit it except when there is disparity in quality—and the owner of the good quality will not be satisfied with matching his good [grain] with its like of the inferior. As for good [grain] in exchange for two inferior ones, that may indeed be intended. But since foods are among the necessities, and the good equals the inferior in the fundamental benefit while differing from it in aspects of luxury, the Sacred Law dropped the aim of luxury in what is the very staff of life. Such is the wisdom of the Sacred Law in prohibiting usury (ribā). And this has become unveiled to us after turning away from the art of jurisprudence (fiqh). Let us, then, annex this to the art of juristic matters, for it is more fitting than all that we have adduced in the controversial questions. And by this, the preponderance of the school of al-Shāfiʿī—may Allah have mercy on him—becomes clear in specifying foods rather than measured commodities (makīlāt); for were gypsum (jiss) to enter into it, then garments and beasts would be even more deserving of entry. And were it not for salt, the school of Mālik—may Allah have mercy on him—would be the most upright of schools therein, for he specified it by the staple foods (aqwāt). But every meaning that the Sacred Law observes must be delimited by a boundary and a definition. This was possible by the staple food (qūt) and possible by the edible (maṭʿūm). So the Sacred Law deemed the specification by the genus of the edible more fitting for everything that is the necessity of survival. And the specifications of the Sacred Law may encompass peripheral edges where the root meaning impelling the ruling has no force, yet the specification occurs thus by necessity. For were it not delimited, creation would be bewildered in following the very essence of the meaning, given its variation with circumstances and persons. For the meaning, in the fullness of its force, varies with circumstances and persons, and thus the limit becomes necessary. Therefore, Allah—exalted is He—said: {«And whosoever transgresses the limits of Allah has indeed wronged his own soul»} (Q 65:1). And because the roots of these meanings do not differ across the Sacred Laws; they differ only in the modes of specification. Just as the law of Jesus son of Mary—peace be upon him—delimited the prohibition of wine by intoxication, while our law has delimited it by being of the genus of the intoxicant, because its small amount invites to its large amount. And what enters within the limits enters into the prohibition by virtue of the genus, just as the root meaning entered by the primary totality. This, then, is a single example of a hidden wisdom among the wisdoms of the two precious metals (naqdayn). One ought to weigh gratitude for the blessing and ingratitude against it by this example. For everything created for a wisdom ought not to be diverted from it—and none knows this save one who has known the wisdom. {«And whosoever is given wisdom has indeed been given abundant good»} (Q 2:269). But the jewels of wisdom do not alight upon hearts that are dunghills of lusts and playgrounds of devils. Rather, none remembers save those possessed of minds. Therefore, he—peace and blessings be upon him—said: \"Were it not that the devils hover about the hearts of the sons of Adam, they would behold the kingdom of the heavens.\" And when you have known this example, then measure upon it your movement and your stillness, your speech and your silence, and every act issuing from you—for it is either gratitude or ingratitude, since it is inconceivable that it be free of both. Some of that we describe in the tongue of jurisprudence, which the common folk articulate, as \"disliked\" (makrūh), and some as \"perilous\" (khaṭar). But all of that, with the people of hearts, is described as perilous. So I say, for instance: If you cleansed yourself with the right hand, you have been ungrateful for the blessing of the two hands. For Allah created for you the two hands and made one of them stronger than the other. The stronger one, by its greater preponderance in most cases, deserves honor and preference. And preferring the deficient is a deviation from justice—and Allah does not command injustice. Then He who gave you the hands made you needful of acts, some of which are noble, like taking the Muṣḥaf, and some base, like removing filth. So if you take the Muṣḥaf with the left hand and remove filth with the right, you have singled out the noble for what is base, thereby diminishing its due, wronging it, and deviating from justice. Likewise, if you spat, for instance, toward the direction of the Qiblah, or faced it in relieving yourself, you have been ungrateful for the blessing of Allah—exalted is He—in the creation of the directions and the creation of the world's expanse. For He created the directions to be your expanse in movement, and He divided the directions into those He did not honor and those He honored, by placing therein a House He attributed to Himself, as an inclination [toward His servants].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 985", "section_title_en": "The Wisdom Behind the Prohibition of Usury (Ribā) and the Principle of Proportional Gratitude for Divine Blessings", "section_index": 985, "anchors": "- Root: طعم (ṭaʿima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning pertains to tasting, flavor, and by extension that which is eaten—food as sustenance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, al-ṭaʿām denotes the essential nutriment preserving life (qūt), distinguished from mere luxury; the Sharīʿah's specification by \"food\" (ṭaʿām) rather than \"measured commodities\" (makīlāt) reflects a divine intent tied to necessity, not convention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense is edible substance; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to what \"nourishes\" a purpose, e.g., a legal cause (ʿillah) sustaining a ruling.\n- Root: حدد (ḥaddada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To set a boundary, define a limit, or fix a precise measure; al-ḥadd is the terminal edge separating one thing from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ḥudūd are the formal definitions and boundaries set by divine command; they may encompass peripheral cases where the underlying rationale (maʿnā) alone cannot determine the judgment, necessitating a fixed limit for practical guidance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical demarcation (e.g., land boundaries); metaphorical: legal specification (takhṣīṣ) by the Lawgiver, where the limit is a divine convention (tawqīf) beyond pure rational analogy.\n- Root: كفر (kafara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, conceal, or deny; also to be ungrateful for a benefit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kufrān (ingratitude) is the antithesis of shukr (gratitude); it entails misusing a blessing against its intended purpose, thereby \"covering\" the divine beneficence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a seed with earth; metaphorical: veiling the soul from recognizing and honoring the Giver by diverting His gifts from their ordained ends.\n- Root: شكر (shakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To acknowledge a favor and respond with praise or appropriate action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True gratitude (shukr) is the soul's recognition of the blessing, the heart's delight in the Giver, and the bodily members' employment of the gift in obedience to its purpose.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: praise for a benefit; metaphorical: the actual \"spending\" of a blessing in its rightful place, as the tongue's thanks is but a token of the limb's due.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Specification with \"innamā\") — Used to confine a predicate to a subject, here restricting the legal cause of ribā to foodstuffs, and the principle of gratitude to every act, whether motion or stillness.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قال ابن مسعود في قوله تعالى {ولا يخرجن إلا أن يأتين بفاحشة مبينة} مهما بذت على أهله وآذت زوجها فهو فاحشة وهذا أريد به في العدة ولكنه تنبيه على المقصود وإن كان الأذى من الزوج فلها أن تفتدي ببذل مال يكره للرجل أن يأخذ منها أكثر مما أعطى فإن ذلك إجحاف بها وتحامل عليها وتجارة على البضع قال تعالى {فلا جناح عليهما فيما افتدت به} فرد ما أخذته فما دونه لائق بالفداء فإن سألت الطلاق بغير ما بأس فهي آثمة قال صلى الله عليه وسلم أيما امرأة سألت زوجها طلاقها من غير ما بأس لم ترح رائحة الجنة (3) وفي لفظ آخر فالجنة عليها", "english": "**The third:** Fear of excessive hardship (ḥaraj) due to the multitude of children, and guarding against the need for toil in earning a livelihood and entering into evil means of acquisition. This also is not prohibited, for the paucity of hardship assists one in religion. Indeed, perfection and excellence lie in trust in God (tawakkul) and confidence in God's guarantee, where He said: {«وما من دابة في الأرض إلا على الله رزقها»} \"And there is no creature on earth but that upon Allah is its provision\" [Hūd: 6]. Without doubt, in this [trust] there is a falling from the summit of perfection and an abandonment of what is more excellent — but looking to consequences and preserving wealth and storing it up, despite its contradicting tawakkul, we do not say it is prohibited.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Marriage", "section_title_ar": "Section 307", "section_title_en": "** On Coitus Interruptus ('Azl), Its Intentions, and the Etiquettes of Childbirth and Divorce", "section_index": 307, "anchors": "**\n- Root: عزل (ʿazl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Separation, removal, setting aside\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Withdrawal to prevent conception\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical separation; Metaphorical: preventing pregnancy\n- Root: وأد (waʾad)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burying a female infant alive (pre-Islamic practice)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The killing of daughters by burial\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: burial alive; Metaphorical: any prevention of life\n- Root: طلق (ṭalaq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Divorce; dissolution of marriage bond\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Legal dissolution of the marriage contract\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing/loosing; Metaphorical: dissolution of marriage\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional clauses with \"فإن قلت\" (objection-response dialectic structure)\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "2": [{"arabic": "الثالثة أن ينظر فيما يأخذه فإن لم يكن من حل تورع عنه {ومن يتق الله يجعل له مخرجا ويرزقه من حيث لا يحتسب} ولن يعدم المتورع عن الحرام فتوحا من الحلال فلا يأخذ من أموال الأتراك والجنود وعمال السلاطين ومن أكثر كسبه من الحرام إلا إذا ضاق الأمر عليه وكان ما يسلم إليه لا يعرف له مالكا معينا فله أن يأخذ بقدر الحاجة فإن فتوى الشرع في مثل هذا أن يتصدق به على ما سيأتي بيانه في كتاب الحلال والحرام وذلك إذا عجز عن الحلال فإذا أخذ لم يكن أخذه أخذ زكاة إذ لا يقع زكاة عن مؤديه وهو حرام الرابعة أن يتوقى مواقع الريبة والاشتباه في مقدار ما يأخذه فلا يأخذ إلا المقدار", "english": "The second: that he thank the giver, supplicate for him, and praise him; and his gratitude and supplication should be such that it does not remove him from being a mere intermediary, but rather he is a channel for the arrival of God's blessing to him. And the channel has a right, insofar as God made him a channel and an intermediary — and that does not negate seeing the blessing as from God, Glorified be He. For he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"He who does not thank the people has not thanked God\" {«من لم يشكر الناس لم يشكر الله»}. And God, Mighty and Majestic, has praised His servants in places for their deeds, while He is their Creator and the Originator of the capacity for them — such as His saying, Exalted is He: {«نعم العبد إنه أواب»} [\"Excellent is the servant. Indeed, he is penitent.\"] — and other similar instances.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Zakat", "section_title_ar": "Section 179", "section_title_en": "The Etiquette of the Recipient of Wealth: On the Virtue of Poverty and the Conduct of Taking from Others", "section_index": 179, "anchors": "- Root: كرم (karuma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: nobility, generosity, honorableness of origin and character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: Divine Generosity (al-Karam) as an attribute of God entailing beneficence (al-Ifḍāl) that is pure grace, not obligation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: nobility of lineage/character. Metaphorical: God's abundant giving without recompense.\n- Root: فضل (faḍala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: surplus, excess, superiority, that which remains beyond need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: God's Bounty (al-Faḍl) as gratuitous favor exceeding what is merited; distinct from justice ('Adl).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: excess/surplus. Metaphorical: divine favor and grace.\n- Root: نعم (ni'ma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: well-being, bounty, benefaction; state of goodness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-Ni'ma as the good state (al-ḥālah al-ḥasanah) bestowed by God; its genus encompasses both little and much.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pleasant life and bounty. Metaphorical: \"خفت نعامتهم\" (their ostriches departed) — i.e., they went away, indicating the fleeting nature of worldly boons.\n- Root: قدر (qadara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: measure, extent, power, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-Qudrah for God is the negation of incapacity; for man, a capacity for specific acts. Al-Qadr: the divine measure/decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measure, amount, power. Metaphorical: \"فرس بعيد القدر\" (a horse of far-reaching stride) — extended reach.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Governing the precise restriction of agency and causation in statements like \"إنما المنكر أن يرى الواسطة أصلا\" (The only objectionable thing is to regard the intermediary as the origin).", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Zakat'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "الأول تعجيل الطعام فذلك من إكرام الضيف وقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم من كان يؤمن بالله واليوم الآخر فليكرم ضيفه (1) ومهما حضر الأكثرون وغاب واحد أو اثنان وتأخروا عن الوقت الموعود فحق الحاضر في التعجيل أولى من حق أولئك في التأخير إلا أن يكون المتأخر فقيرا أو ينكسر قلبه بذلك فلا بأس في التأخير وأحد المعنيين في قوله تعالى {هل أتاك حديث ضيف إبراهيم المكرمين} أنهم أكرموا بتعجيل الطعام إليهم دل عليه قوله تعالى {فما لبث أن جاء بعجل حنيذ} وقوله {فراغ إلى أهله فجاء بعجل سمين} والروغان الذهاب بسرعة وقيل في خفية وقيل جاء بفخذ من لحم وإنما سمي ع", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Do not burden your guest so that you make him hate you, for whoever hates his guest has hated Allah, and whoever hates Allah, Allah hates him.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 290", "section_title_en": "The Etiquettes of Hospitality: Invitation, Response, Attendance, and the Serving of Food — Section 290 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 290, "anchors": "- Root: ضيف (ḍ-y-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to incline, to lean toward.\" The guest (al-ḍayf) is one who inclines toward you, alighting as a visitor; the root carries the sense of leaning or bending.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The guest is \"one who inclines to you, alighting with you\"; hospitality (al-ḍiyāfah) became customary in the sense of receiving such a one. The word is originally a verbal noun, hence one and many are equal in most usage.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"ḍāfa ilayhi: he inclined toward him\"; \"ḍāfat al-shams: the sun inclined toward setting.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"aḍāfa ilayhi amran: he attributed a matter to him, leaning it upon him\"; \"al-muḍāf: the one constrained, hemmed in, in distress.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the Prophet's statement \"innamā hādhihi al-akhlāq bi-yad Allāh\" (these morals are only in the hand of Allah), where innamā restricts the attribution of moral character exclusively to divine bestowal.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "3": [{"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم يا أبا هريرة مر أهلك بالصلاة فإن الله يأتيك بالرزق من حيث لا تحتسب (13) وقال بعض العلماء مثل المصلي مثل التاجر الذي لا يحصل له الربح حتى يخلص له رأس المال وكذلك المصلي لا تقبل له نافلة حتى يؤدي الفريضة وكان أبو بكر رضي الله عنه يقول إذا حضرت الصلاة قوموا إلى ناركم التي أوقدتموها فأطفئوها فضيلة إتمام الأركان قال صلى الله عليه وسلم مثل الصلاة المكتوبة كمثل الميزان من أوفى استوفى (14) وقال يزيد الرقاشي كانت صلاة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مستوية كأنها موزونة (1) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الرجلين من أمتي ليقومان إلى الصلاة وركوعهما وسجودهما وا", "english": "I say: Now, the five prayers extinguish sins just as water extinguishes filth (1). And the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Indeed, the prayers are an expiation for what occurs between them, so long as the major sins are avoided\" (2). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Between us and the hypocrites is the witnessing of the night prayer (al-ʿatamah) and the dawn prayer; they cannot bear them\" (3). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Whoever meets God while neglecting his prayer, God will not regard any of his good deeds\" (4). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Prayer is the pillar of the religion; whoever abandons it has demolished the religion\" (5). And he was asked, peace and blessings be upon him, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" He said: \"Prayer at its appointed times\" (6). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Whoever preserves the five prayers, perfecting their purification and their appointed times, they shall be for him a light and a proof on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever neglects them shall be gathered with Pharaoh and Hāmān\" (7). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"The key to Paradise is prayer\" (8). And he said: \"God has not obligated upon His creation, after monotheism (tawḥīd), anything more beloved to Him than prayer; and had there been anything more beloved to Him, His angels would have devoted themselves to it — among them are those who bow (rākiʿ), those who prostrate (sājid), and those who stand and sit\" (9). And the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Whoever deliberately abandons a prayer has disbelieved\" (10) — meaning: he has nearly been uprooted from faith by the unraveling of its rope and the collapse of its pillar, just as one who has nearly reached a town is said to have arrived at it and entered it. And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"Whoever deliberately abandons a prayer has absolved himself of the covenant of Muhammad, peace be upon him\" (11). And Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, said: \"Whoever performs ablution and perfects it, then sets out deliberately for prayer, he is in a state of prayer as long as he intends the prayer; and for each of his steps, a good deed is recorded for him, and by the other, a sin is erased. So when any of you hears the call to commence (iqāmah), he should not delay — for the one with the greatest reward among you is the one whose dwelling is farthest.\" They said: \"Why, O Abū Hurayrah?\" He said: \"Because of the abundance of steps.\" And it is narrated: \"Indeed, the first thing examined of a servant's deeds on the Day of Resurrection is the prayer. If it is found complete, it is accepted from him, along with the rest of his deeds; and if it is found deficient, it is rejected upon him, along with the rest of his deeds\" (12). And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"O Abū Hurayrah, command your family to pray, for God shall bring you sustenance from whence you do not reckon\" (13). And some scholars said: The likeness of the one who prays is the likeness of a merchant who gains no profit until his capital is secured; likewise, the one who prays has no supererogatory prayer accepted until he has performed the obligatory one.", "book_title": "Mysteries of Prayer", "section_title_ar": "Section 121", "section_title_en": "Section 121 — The Virtue of Prayer and the Completion of Its Pillars", "section_index": 121, "anchors": "- Root: صلو (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes supplication, blessing, and the act of prayer; the ﷽ prayer (ṣalāh) as the established ritual worship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh in the theological sense is the act of drawing near to God through devoted worship, encompassing both the physical postures and the inward humility (khushūʿ).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the ritual prayer with its prescribed bowings and prostrations; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to supplication and divine blessing.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The syntactic structure allows for separation between the preposition and its object to emphasize rhetorical weight, as in the prophetic narrations where conditions and exceptions are interposed for clarity.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "نسينا ربما عرض من حيث لا نحتسب وإن تجردنا لذكره كنا قد أهملنا ذكر الله فالجمع أولى وقال العلماء المحققون غلط الفريقان أما الأول فقد تجرد لذكر الشيطان ونسي ذكر الله فلا يخفى غلطه وإنما أمرنا بالحذر من الشيطان كيلا يصدنا عن الذكر فكيف نجعل ذكره اغلب الأشياء على قلوبنا وهو منتهى ضرر العدو ثم يؤدي ذلك إلى خلو القلب عن نور ذكر الله تعالى فإذا قصد الشيطان مثل هذا القلب وليس فيه نور ذكر الله تعالى وقوة الاشتغال به فيوشك أن يظفر به ولا يقوى على دفعه فلم يأمرنا بانتظار الشيطان ولا بإد", "english": "...and the world, which is the source of trials and tribulations, the mine of forbidden pleasures and lusts. And Moses, peace be upon him, said—as God Most High has related concerning him—{«هذا من عمل الشيطان»} (\"This is of Satan's doing\" — Qur'an 28:15). For this reason, God warned all of creation against him, saying: {«يا بني آدم لا يفتننكم الشيطان كما أخرج أبويكم من الجنة»} (\"O children of Adam, let not Satan tempt you as he brought your parents out of Paradise\" — Qur'an 7:27). And He, Glorified and Exalted, said: {«إنه يراكم هو وقبيله من حيث لا ترونهم»} (\"Indeed, he sees you, he and his tribe, from where you do not see them\" — Qur'an 7:27). The Qur'an, from its beginning to its end, is a warning against Satan. So how could one claim security from him? And taking precaution against him, in the manner God has commanded, does not contradict occupation with the love of God. For part of loving Him is obeying His command. He has commanded vigilance against the enemy just as He commanded vigilance against the disbelievers, saying: {«وليعأخذوا حذرهم وأسلحتهم»} (\"And let them take their precautions and their weapons\" — Qur'an 4:102), and saying: {«وأعدوا لهم ما استطعتم من قوة ومن رباط الخيل»} (\"And prepare against them whatever you are able of power and of steeds of war\" — Qur'an 8:60). So if, by God's command, you are obligated to be wary of a disbelieving enemy whom you can see, then it is all the more fitting that you be obligated to be wary of an enemy who sees you while you do not see him.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Status and Ostentation", "section_title_ar": "Section 830", "section_title_en": "On the Necessity of Vigilance Against Satan and Its Harmony with Trust in God and Remembrance of Him", "section_index": 830, "anchors": "- Root: شيط (Shayṭ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes burning, scorching, or spoiling (as meat over a fire), and by extension, perishing or being ruined.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشيطان\" (Satan) is derived from this root, signifying one who is burned, ruined, or cast away from mercy—the archetype of the adversary who destroys through temptation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"شاط دمه\" — his blood went in vain (was wasted). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"أشاط السلطان دمه\" — the ruler declared his blood permissible (wasted). Thus, Satan is the one who causes spiritual ruin and waste.\n- Root: حذر (Ḥadhar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Caution, wariness, and taking precautions against a feared harm.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحذر: احتراز من مخيف\" — vigilance is a protective withdrawal from what is frightening. It is a commanded state, as in {«خذوا حذركم»} (Take your precautions — Qur'an 4:71).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"حذرته وحاذرته\" — I was wary of him. Metaphorical: \"حذار من أرماحنا حذار\" — a poetic imperative to beware. Vigilance is both a physical and spiritual act of self-preservation.\n- Root: قلب (Qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or flip something from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — to turn a thing from its face. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems. The heart's occupation is decisive: it turns either toward God or toward the enemy.\n- Root: وكل (Wakal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To entrust, delegate, or rely upon another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التوكيل: أن تعتمد على غيرك وتجعله نائبا عنك\" — trust (tawakkul) is reliance upon another as your deputy. God says: {«وكفى بالله وكيلا»} (And sufficient is Allah as Disposer of affairs — Qur'an 4:81).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وكل إليه الأمر\" — he delegated the matter to him. Metaphorical: \"فلان وكل\" — a weak man who leans on others. True tawakkul does not negate taking means; it purifies the heart's reliance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"إنما\" and restrictive constructions (e.g., \"ليس ... إلا\") are used to delimit predicates, as in the author's argument that vigilance is not a contradiction of love or trust but a fulfillment of the divine command.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "يجعل له مخرجا ويرزقه من حيث لا يحتسب} ولن يعدم المتورع عن الحرام فتوحا من الحلال فلا يأخذ من أموال الأتراك والجنود وعمال السلاطين ومن أكثر كسبه من الحرام إلا إذا ضاق الأمر عليه وكان ما يسلم إليه لا يعرف له مالكا معينا فله أن يأخذ بقدر الحاجة فإن فتوى الشرع في مثل هذا أن يتصدق به على ما سيأتي بيانه في كتاب الحلال والحرام وذلك إذا عجز عن الحلال فإذا أخذ لم يكن أخذه أخذ زكاة إذ لا يقع زكاة عن مؤديه وهو حرام الرابعة أن يتوقى مواقع الريبة والاشتباه في مقدار ما يأخذه فلا يأخذ إلا المقد", "english": "The second: that he thank the giver, supplicate for him, and praise him; and his gratitude and supplication should be such that it does not remove him from being a mere intermediary, but rather he is a channel for the arrival of God's blessing to him. And the channel has a right, insofar as God made him a channel and an intermediary — and that does not negate seeing the blessing as from God, Glorified be He. For he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"He who does not thank the people has not thanked God\" {«من لم يشكر الناس لم يشكر الله»}. And God, Mighty and Majestic, has praised His servants in places for their deeds, while He is their Creator and the Originator of the capacity for them — such as His saying, Exalted is He: {«نعم العبد إنه أواب»} [\"Excellent is the servant. Indeed, he is penitent.\"] — and other similar instances.", "book_title": "Disciplining the Soul", "section_title_ar": "Section 179", "section_title_en": "The Etiquette of the Recipient of Wealth: On the Virtue of Poverty and the Conduct of Taking from Others", "section_index": 179, "anchors": "- Root: كرم (karuma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: nobility, generosity, honorableness of origin and character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: Divine Generosity (al-Karam) as an attribute of God entailing beneficence (al-Ifḍāl) that is pure grace, not obligation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: nobility of lineage/character. Metaphorical: God's abundant giving without recompense.\n- Root: فضل (faḍala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: surplus, excess, superiority, that which remains beyond need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: God's Bounty (al-Faḍl) as gratuitous favor exceeding what is merited; distinct from justice ('Adl).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: excess/surplus. Metaphorical: divine favor and grace.\n- Root: نعم (ni'ma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: well-being, bounty, benefaction; state of goodness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-Ni'ma as the good state (al-ḥālah al-ḥasanah) bestowed by God; its genus encompasses both little and much.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pleasant life and bounty. Metaphorical: \"خفت نعامتهم\" (their ostriches departed) — i.e., they went away, indicating the fleeting nature of worldly boons.\n- Root: قدر (qadara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning: measure, extent, power, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-Qudrah for God is the negation of incapacity; for man, a capacity for specific acts. Al-Qadr: the divine measure/decree.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measure, amount, power. Metaphorical: \"فرس بعيد القدر\" (a horse of far-reaching stride) — extended reach.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Governing the precise restriction of agency and causation in statements like \"إنما المنكر أن يرى الواسطة أصلا\" (The only objectionable thing is to regard the intermediary as the origin).", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Disciplining the Soul'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "قوله تعالى ومن يتوكل على الله فهو حسبه فتوكلت على الله عز وجل فهو حسبي قال شقيق يا حاتم وفقك الله تعالى فإني نظرت في علوم التوراة والإنجيل والزبور والفرقان العظيم فوجدت جميع أنواع الخير والديانة وهي تدور على هذه الثمان مسائل فمن استعملها فقد استعمل الكتب الأربعة فهذا الفن من العلم لا يهتم بإدراكه والتفطن له إلا علماء الآخرة فأما علماء الدنيا فيشتغلون بما يتيسر به اكتساب المال والجاه ويهملون أمثال هذه العلوم التي بعث الله بها الأنبياء كلهم عليهم السلام وقال الضحاك بن مزاحم أدركتهم و", "english": "Know that the scholar who does not act upon his knowledge is condemned by the Book, the Sunnah, and the sayings of the sages. God, exalted be He, said: {«يا أيها الذين آمنوا لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون * كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون»} (\"O you who believe, why do you say what you do not do? Most hateful it is to God that you say what you do not do\"). And He said: {«أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم»} (\"Do you command people to righteousness while you forget yourselves?\"). And He said: {«ومن الناس من يعجبك قوله في الحياة الدنيا ويشهد الله على ما في قلبه وهو ألد الخصام»} (\"And among people is he whose speech pleases you in the worldly life, and he calls God to witness what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents\").", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 54", "section_title_en": "Section 54", "section_index": 54, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "عز وجل كفاه الله تعالى ما أهمه ورزقه من حيث لا يحتسب (7) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أوحى الله عز وجل إلى إبراهيم عليه السلام يا إبراهيم إني عليم أحب كل عليم (8) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم العالم أمين الله سبحانه في الأرض (9) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم صنفان من أمتي إذا صلحوا صلح الناس وإذا فسدوا فسد الناس الأمراء والفقهاء (10) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا أتى علي يوم لا أزداد فيه علما يقربني إلى الله عز وجل فلا بورك لي في طلوع شمس ذلك اليوم (11) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم في تفضيل العلم على العبادة والشها", "english": "How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 4", "section_title_en": "The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars", "section_index": 4, "anchors": "- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Insight"}], "7": [{"arabic": "هذا الرجل واجب أن يقضى من مال بيت المال ومن الزكاة وقد قال تعالى ومن قدر عليه رزقه فلينفق مما آتاه الله قيل معناه ليبع أحد ثوبيه وقيل معناه فليستقرض بجاهه فذلك مما آتاه الله وقال بعضهم إن لله تعالى عبادا ينفقون على قدر بضائعهم ولله عباد ينفقون على قدر حسن الظن بالله تعالى ومات بعضهم فأوصى بماله لثلاث طوائف الأقوياء والأسخياء والأغنياء فقيل من هؤلاء فقال أما الأقوياء فهم أهل التوكل على الله تعالى وأما الأسخياء فهم أهل حسن الظن بالله تعالى وأما الأغنياء فهم أهل الانقطاع إلى الله تعالى فإذن مهما وجدت هذه الشروط فيه وفي المال وفي المعطي فليأخ", "english": "And one of those residing near the Sacred House [in Mecca] said: I had with me some dirhams which I had set aside for spending in the path of God. Then I heard a poor man who had finished his circumambulation (tawaf) saying in a faint voice: \"I am hungry as you see, naked as you see — so what do you see in what you see, O He who sees and is not seen?\" I looked and saw upon him two worn garments that scarcely concealed him. I said to myself: I find no place for my dirhams more excellent than this. So I carried them to him. He looked at them, then took from them five dirhams and said: \"Four are the price of two waist-wrappers, and one dirham I shall spend — three [remain]. I have no need of the rest,\" and he returned them. He said: Then I saw him the second night wearing two new waist-wrappers. Something stirred within me concerning him, so he turned to me, took my hand, and made me circumambulate with him for a week — every circuit of it upon gems from the mines of the earth, crunching beneath our feet up to the ankles — among them gold, silver, ruby, pearl, and precious stone — and this was not shown to the people. He said: \"All of this has been given to me, and I have renounced it, and I take from the hands of creatures — because these are burdens and tribulation, while for the servants therein is mercy and grace.\"", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1080", "section_title_en": "On the Perils of Accepting Excess Wealth and the Discipline of Contentment — Section 1080", "section_index": 1080, "anchors": "- Root: أ-خ-ذ (akhadha)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of taking, seizing, accepting with the hand; implies volitional acquisition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, taking (akhdh) is tied to what is lawfully permitted (halal) versus what carries spiritual liability; the act itself is neutral, its moral weight determined by intention and need.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) — physical taking; metaphorical (majaz) — accepting provision as from God, not from the intermediary agent.\n- Root: ف-ت-ن (fatanah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Trial, affliction, testing; originally from burning (fatn) — the fire that tests gold.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ibtila' (trial) is God's testing of the servant's obedience; the trial is not for God's knowledge but for the servant's self-disclosure and recompense.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — smelting to purify; metaphorical — wealth as a trial that reveals whether one acts with gratitude or ingratitude.\n- Root: ز-ه-د (zuhd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Abstinence, renunciation, regarding something as insignificant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Zuhd is not the absence of wealth but the absence of attachment in the heart; it is a station of the soul, not a condition of the purse.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — turning away from; metaphorical — taking wealth only to discharge it immediately to the needy, which is the zenith of zuhd.\n- Root: ر-ف-ق (rifq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gentleness, ease, kindness; the opposite of harshness (unf).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): God's rifq with the servant is sending provision exactly sufficient to need, without surplus that would burden accountability.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — softness in manner; metaphorical — divine gentleness in apportioning sustenance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Prophet's statement «لا حق لابن آدم إلا في ثلاث» restricts rightful claim exclusively to three categories, and the subsequent \"فما زاد فهو حساب\" is a conditional consequent establishing a universal ruling on surplus.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "11": [{"arabic": "{يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر بتعظيم أهل الدنيا إياهم فإنهم من الخاسرين (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم أول ما خل", "english": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Delusion", "section_title_ar": "Section 65", "section_title_en": "The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions", "section_index": 65, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "12": [{"arabic": "ينزل إليهم من القدر والأمر فقيل خلق سبع سموات ومن الأرض مثلهن يتنزل الأمر بينهن لتعلموا أن الله على كل شيء قدير وأن الله قد أحاط بكل شيء علما وهذه أمور لا يعلم تأويلها إلا الله والراسخون في العلم وعبر ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما عن اختصاص الراسخين في العلم بعلوم لا تحتملها أفهام الخلق حيث قرأ قوله تعالى {يتنزل الأمر بينهن} فقال لو ذكرت ما أعرفه من معنى هذه الآية لرجمتموني وفي لفظ آخر لقلتم إنه كافر ولنقتصر على هذا القدر فقد خرج عنان الكلام عن قبضة الاختيار وامتزج بعلم المعاملة ما ليس منه فلنرجع إلى مقاصد الشكر فنقول إذا رجع حقيقة الشكر إلى كون العبد مستعملا في إتمام حكمة الله تعالى فأشكر العب", "english": "Thus was the beginning of this matter and its end. And do not understand it unless you are worthy of it. And if you are worthy of it, the eye is opened and you see, so you have no need of a leader to lead you. The blind man can indeed be led, but only to a limit. For when the path narrows and becomes sharper than a sword and finer than a hair, the bird is able to fly over it but is not able to drag a blind man behind it. And when the field becomes narrow and subtle — subtle as water, for example — and crossing is possible only by swimming, then the one skilled in the art of swimming may be able to cross by himself, and perhaps he is not able to drag another behind him. These matters bear the same relation to walking upon the way of the common masses of creatures as walking upon water bears to walking upon earth. Swimming can be learned; but as for walking upon water, it is not acquired by instruction, but rather is attained through the strength of certainty (yaqīn). For this reason it was said to the Prophet, peace be upon him, that Jesus, peace be upon him, was said to have walked upon water, and he, peace be upon him, said: \"Had his certainty increased, he would have walked upon the air.\" {«حديث قيل له يقال إن عيسى مشى على الماء قال لو ازداد يقينا لمشي على الهواء»} — This hadith is rejected (munkar) and is not known in this form. What is known is what Ibn Abi al-Dunya narrated in the Book of Certainty from the saying of Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, who said: The disciples lost their Prophet, and it was said to them: \"He has headed toward the sea.\" So they set out seeking him, and when they reached the sea, behold, he was coming toward them walking upon the water. And he mentioned a hadith in which Jesus said: \"If the son of Adam had a hair's breadth of certainty, he would walk upon the water.\" And Abu Mansur al-Daylami narrated in the Musnad al-Firdaws with a weak chain from the hadith of Muadh ibn Jabal: \"If you knew God with true knowledge, you would walk upon the seas, and the mountains would be removed by your supplications.\" These are symbols and allusions to the meaning of aversion (karāhah) and love (maḥabbah), and good pleasure (riḍā) and wrath (ghaḍab), and gratitude (shukr) and ingratitude (kufrān) — more than this is not fitting for the science of practical conduct (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah). And God, exalted be He, struck an example of this to draw it near to the understanding of creatures, since He knew that He created the jinn and mankind only to worship Him, and their worship was the ultimate purpose of wisdom (ghāyat al-ḥikmah) with respect to them. Then He informed that He has two servants: one whom He loves, whose name is Gabriel — the Holy Spirit and the Trustworthy One — and he is with Him beloved, obeyed, trustworthy, and honored; and another whom He hates, whose name is Iblis — the accursed one, reprieved until the Day of Judgment. Then He delegated guidance (irshād) to Gabriel, saying, the Exalted: {«قل نزله روح القدس من ربك بالحق»} \"Say: The Holy Spirit has brought it down from your Lord with truth\" (16:102), and said, the Exalted: {«يلقي الروح من أمره على من يشاء من عباده»} \"He casts the Spirit of His command upon whom He wills of His servants\" (40:15). And He delegated misguidance (ighwāʾ) to Iblis, saying, the Exalted: {«ليضل عن سبيله»} \"That he may lead astray from His path\" (4:119). And ighwāʾ is the halting of servants short of attaining the ultimate purpose of wisdom. So observe how He attributed it to the servant with whom He was wrathful; and guidance (irshād) is the conduct of them toward the ultimate purpose — so observe how He attributed it to the servant whom He loves. And you have an example of this in custom: when a king is in need of one to give him drink and one to cup him and to clean the courtyard of his house of filth, and he has two servants, he does not appoint for cupping and cleaning except the ugliest and meanest of them, and he does not entrust the carrying of drink and perfume except to the best and most complete of them and the most beloved to him. And you should not say: \"This is my act, and his act was not without my act\" — for you have erred when you attributed that to yourself. Rather, He is the One who directed your motive (dāʿiyah) to the specification of the disliked act to the disliked person, and the beloved act to the beloved person, as a completion of justice (ʿadl). For His justice is sometimes completed through matters in which you have no entry, and sometimes it is completed in you — for you also are among His acts. So your motive, your power, your knowledge, your action, and all the other causes of your movements in expression — all of this is His act, which He arranged with justice in an arrangement from which moderate acts issue forth. Except that you see only yourself, and so you suppose that what appears through you in the world of witness (ʿālam al-shahādah) has no cause from the world of the unseen and the dominion (ʿālam al-ghayb wa al-malakūt). Therefore you attribute it to yourself. And you are only like the child who looks at night at the play of the juggler (mushʿabadh) who brings forth figures from behind a curtain that dance and scream and rise and sit, composed of rags that do not move by themselves — rather, they are moved by fine hair-like threads that do not appear in the darkness of night, their ends in the hand of the juggler, who is hidden from the eyes of the children. So they rejoice and marvel, supposing that those rags dance and play and rise and sit. As for the intelligent ones, they know that this is a moving (taḥrīk) and not a self-movement, but perhaps they do not know its detailed modality. And the one who knows some of its detail does not know it as the juggler knows it, to whom the command belongs and in whose hand is the pulling. Likewise, the children of the people of this world — and all creatures are children relative to the scholars — look at these persons (ashkhāṣ) and suppose that they are the movers, and so they attribute [action] to them. And the scholars know that they are moved, except that they do not know the modality of the moving — and they are the majority. Except the knowers (ʿārifūn), the scholars, and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn), for they have perceived, with the sharpness of their sight, fine spider-web threads — nay, much finer than these — suspended from heaven, their ends attached to the persons of the people of the earth. Those threads are not perceived, due to their fineness, by these outward eyes. Then they witnessed the heads of those threads in their suspension-points (manāṭāt) to which they are attached, and they witnessed for those suspension-points handles (maqābiḍ) that are in the hands of the angels who move the heavens. And they also witnessed the angels of the heavens directed toward the bearers of the Throne (ḥamalat al-ʿarsh), awaiting what descends upon them of the command from the presence of Lordship (ḥaḍrat al-rubūbiyyah) — \"they do not disobey God in what He commands them, and they do what they are commanded\" (66:6). And He expressed these witnessed realities in the Qur'an, and it was said: {«وفي السماء رزقكم وما توعدون»} \"And in the heaven is your provision and what you are promised\" (51:22). And He expressed the waiting of the angels of the heavens for what descends upon them of decree and command, and it was said: \"He created seven heavens and of the earth the like of them; {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن لتعلموا أن الله على كل شيء قدير وأن الله قد أحاط بكل شيء علما»} 'the command descends between them, that you may know that God is powerful over all things and that God encompasses all things in knowledge'\" (65:12). These are matters whose interpretation (taʾwīl) none knows except God and those firmly rooted in knowledge (al-rāsikhūn fī al-ʿilm). And Ibn Abbas, may God be pleased with them both, expressed the exclusivity of those firmly rooted in knowledge to sciences that the understandings of creatures cannot bear, when he recited the saying of the Exalted: {«يتنزل الأمر بينهن»} \"The command descends between them,\" and said: \"Were I to mention what I know of the meaning of this verse, you would stone me\" — and in another wording: \"You would say he is an unbeliever.\"", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 990", "section_title_en": "On the Degrees of Proximity to God and the Hierarchy of Those Who Perfect Divine Wisdom", "section_index": 990, "anchors": "- Root: سير (s-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السير: الذهاب والسفر، وسار يسير سيرا ومسيرا، والسيارة: الرفقة المسافرة\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السير: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان، ويستعار للسلوك في الدين والطريقة\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"سار القوم: مشوا\" — [Metaphorical] \"سار فيه السير: مضى في الأمر، وسار ذكره: انتشر، ومنه: سيرة فلان: طريقته\"\n- Root: مشي (m-sh-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المشي: تردد الأركان على الأرض، والماشية: الإبل والغنم لانتقالها في المرعى\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المشي: الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان بإرادة، ويكنى به عن النميمة، وعن شرب المسهل\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"مشيت وتمشيت، وهي حسنة المشية\" — [Metaphorical] \"مشى بطنه، وأمشاه الدواء، واستمشيت بالدواء، ومنه: مشت المرأة: كثرت أولادها\"\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخلق: التقدير، والخلق: الناس، والخليقة: الطبيعة، والخلق: السجية\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم، ويستعمل في إبداع الشيء من غير أصل ولا احتذاء\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"خلق الخراز الأديم: قدره قبل القطع\" — [Metaphorical] \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\"\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النفس: الروح، والذات، والشيء النفيس\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح، وتطلق على الذات، قال تعالى: {ويحذركم الله نفسه} فنفسه: ذاته\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal] \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — [Metaphorical] \"دفق نفسه أي دمه، وأصابته نفس: عين\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to distinguish exclusive attribution of actions to God versus creatures, as in \"إنما أنت مثل الصبي\" (You are only like a child), restricting the simile to the creature while preserving divine agency.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما في قوله عز وجل الله الذي خلق سبع سموات ومن الأرض مثلهن يتنزل الأمر بينهن لو ذكرت تفسيره لرجمتموني وفي لفظ آخر لقلتم إنه كافر وقال أبو هريرة رضي الله عنه حفظت من رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وعاءين أما أحدهما فبثثته وأما الآخر لو بثثته لقطع هذا الحلقوم وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم ما فضلكم أبو بكر بكثرة صيام ولا صلاة ولكن بسر وقر في صدره (3) رضي الله عنه ولا شك في أن ذلك السر كان متعلقا بقواعد الدين غير خارج منها وما كان من قواعد الدين لم يكن خافيا بظواهره على غيره وقال سهل التستري رضي الله عنه للعالم ثلاثة علوم علم ظاهر", "english": "**Know** that the division of these sciences into the hidden (khafiyyah) and the evident (jaliyyah) is not denied by any possessor of insight (baṣīrah). Only the deficient deny it — those who snatched up something in the earliest days of youth and then froze upon it, having no ascent to the summit of sublimity (shaʾw al-ʿalāʾ) nor to the stations of the scholars and the saints (awliyāʾ). And this is evident from the proofs of the Sacred Law. The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«إن للقرآن ظاهرًا وباطنًا وحدًّا ومطلعًا»} — \"Indeed, the Qurʾān has an outer aspect, an inner aspect, a boundary, and an ascent.\" And ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said — pointing to his breast — \"Indeed, here lie abundant sciences, if only they found carriers to bear them.\"", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 81", "section_title_en": "** Section 81 — On the Outer and Inner Dimensions of Knowledge: The Distinction Between Apparent and Hidden Sciences", "section_index": 81, "anchors": "**\n- Root: ظهر (ẓahara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"ظهر للناس غَيْره\" — to become manifest, apparent, visible; the back (ẓahr) as the outer, visible aspect of a thing\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ẓāhir is that which is evident and perceptible; used in contrast to bāṭin (inner), establishing the epistemological distinction between exoteric and esoteric dimensions\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — \"ظهر الأمر: وضح واتضح\" (literal: became clear); Majāz — \"قلبت الأمر ظهرا لبطن\" (metaphorical: turned the matter over, examining both its outer and inner aspects)\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of knowledge, perception, and discernment; the mark or sign (ʿalam) by which something is known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; two types: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what is or is not attributed to it\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as that which is sought and pursued\n- Root: سر (sirr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The secret, the concealed; that which is hidden in the depths of the heart (sirr as the innermost faculty)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The sirr is that which is concealed from others; in theological context, the inner reality known only to God and His elect\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinction between what is publicly declared (ʿalāniyah) and what is privately concealed (sirr)\n- Root: شرع (sharaʿa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشرع: نهج الطريق الواضح\" — the clear, open path; then applied metaphorically to the divine Law (Sharīʿah) as the way prescribed by God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqīqah — the clear path to water; Majāz — the divine Law as the path to salvation\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"إنما ينكرها القاصرون\" (only the deficient deny it), establishing exclusive attribution\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "أعلم أن الله على كل شيء قدير وأن الله قد أحاط بكل شيء علما وأحصى كل شيء عددا اللهم إني أعوذ بك من شر نفسي ومن شر كل دابة أنت آخذ بناصيتها إن ربي على صراط مستقيم (1) دعاء الخليل إبراهيم عليه الصلاة والسلام كان يقول إذا أصبح اللهم إن هذا خلق جديد فافتحه علي بطاعتك واختمه لي بمغفرتك ورضوانك وارزقني فيه حسنة تقبلها مني وزكها وضعفها لي وما عملت فيه من سيئة فاغفرها لي إنك غفور رحيم ودود كريم قال ومن دعا بهذا الدعاء إذا أصبح فقد أدى شكر يومه دعاء عيسى عليه الصلاة والسلام كان يقول اللهم إ", "english": "And it is related that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to him: \"O Buraydah, shall I not teach you words which, if anyone desires good through them, he is taught them, and then he never forgets them ever?\" He said: \"I said, 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.'\" He said: \"Say: 'O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen my weakness in Your pleasure, and take me by my forelock toward goodness, and make Islam the limit of my contentment. O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen me; and I am abased, so honor me; and I am poor, so enrich me, O Most Merciful of the merciful.'\" (2)", "book_title": "On Invocations and Supplications", "section_title_ar": "Section 248", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 248: Supplications of the Companions and Prophets (Peace Be Upon Them)", "section_index": 248, "anchors": "- Root: دعو (d-'-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of calling, invoking, summoning; du'a as the act of calling upon God with humble petition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance of du'a as the essence of worship ('ibadah), the creature's acknowledgment of need (iftaqar) before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) — to call out; Metaphorical (majaz) — the soul's inward turning to God, transcending mere vocal utterance.\n- Root: وقي (w-q-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To protect, guard, preserve from harm; the root of taqwa (God-consciousness) and ittiqa (self-preservation from divine displeasure).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological state of being shielded by God's grace through the performance of obligations and abstention from prohibitions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical protection; Metaphorical — spiritual safeguarding of the heart from worldly attachment.\n- Root: حول (h-w-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Power, might, capacity to change or transform; the ability to effect motion or cessation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the formula \"لا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله\" (lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh), it denotes the creature's absolute incapacity and total reliance on divine power (quwwah) for all acts of obedience and avoidance of sin.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical strength; Metaphorical — the spiritual power granted by God to overcome one's lower self (nafs).\n- Root: فقر (f-q-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Poverty, neediness, lack of possessions; the state of one who requires what he does not possess.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ontological poverty of all creatures before God — \"يا أيها الناس أنتم الفقراء إلى الله\" {O mankind, you are the poor in need of Allah} [Fatir: 15]. This is the existential need that defines creaturehood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — material poverty; Metaphorical — the soul's recognition of its utter dependence on God for guidance, sustenance, and salvation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object when the intervening phrase constitutes a complete and meaningful utterance, as seen in the extended invocations where multiple clauses are joined by conjunctions (wa) before the governing verb's object is completed.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'", "badge": "Du'a"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 3220], [2, 3220, 5254], [3, 5254, 6330], [4, 6330, 8137], [5, 8137, 10843], [6, 10843, 13629], [7, 13629, 15918], [8, 15918, 18180], [9, 18180, 19573], [10, 19573, 21738], [11, 21738, 22828], [12, 22828, 24138], [13, 24138, 24855], [14, 24855, 27324], [15, 27324, 27876], [16, 27876, 30179], [17, 30179, 31310], [18, 31310, 32924], [19, 32924, 34885], [20, 34885, 35492], [21, 35492, 37202], [22, 37202, 38774], [23, 38774, 40946], [24, 40946, 42187], [25, 42187, 43456], [26, 43456, 44546], [27, 44546, 45511], [28, 45511, 47014], [29, 47014, 48283], [30, 48283, 49373], [31, 49373, 50325], [32, 50325, 51235], [33, 51235, 52338], [34, 52338, 53055], [35, 53055, 54365], [36, 54365, 55455], [37, 55455, 56545], [38, 56545, 57841], [39, 57841, 58724], [40, 58724, 59761], [41, 59761, 61085]], "2": [[1, 400, 1701], [2, 1701, 2790], [3, 2790, 4530], [4, 4530, 7226], [5, 7226, 9006], [6, 9006, 9723], [7, 9723, 12286], [8, 12286, 14066], [9, 14066, 16430], [10, 16430, 17519], [11, 17519, 18435], [12, 18435, 19883], [13, 19883, 22406], [14, 22406, 24170], [15, 24170, 25139], [16, 25139, 26403], [17, 26403, 27864], [18, 27864, 28369], [19, 28369, 28900], [20, 28900, 29856], [21, 29856, 31131], [22, 31131, 32446], [23, 32446, 34252], [24, 34252, 35793], [25, 35793, 36723], [26, 36723, 37905], [27, 37905, 38954], [28, 38954, 40269], [29, 40269, 40973], [30, 40973, 42486]], "3": [[1, 400, 2402], [2, 2402, 2943], [3, 2943, 3944], [4, 3944, 4648], [5, 4648, 6190], [6, 6190, 7137], [7, 7137, 8964], [8, 8964, 9965], [9, 9965, 11034], [10, 11034, 11954], [11, 11954, 13453], [12, 13453, 14441], [13, 14441, 15510], [14, 15510, 16511], [15, 16511, 17620], [16, 17620, 18283], [17, 18283, 19149], [18, 19149, 20218], [19, 20218, 21233], [20, 21233, 22180], [21, 22180, 23547]], "4": [[1, 400, 2747], [2, 2747, 4158], [3, 4158, 4734], [4, 4734, 6519], [5, 6519, 7095], [6, 7095, 10265], [7, 10265, 10898], [8, 10898, 12496], [9, 12496, 14886], [10, 14886, 16211], [11, 16211, 17449], [12, 17449, 19796], [13, 19796, 20372], [14, 20372, 21783], [15, 21783, 23741], [16, 23741, 25814], [17, 25814, 27700], [18, 27700, 28333], [19, 28333, 2977